<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<section xml:id="num-operations" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Operations with Natural Numbers</title>

  <objectives>
    <title>Learning Outcomes</title>
    <ul>
      <li><xref ref="lo-n-2" /> — Represent operations on a number line</li>
      <li><xref ref="lo-n-6" /> — Properties of arithmetic</li>
      <li><xref ref="lo-u-3" /> — Select appropriate methods</li>
    </ul>
  </objectives>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      The four basic operations — addition, subtraction, multiplication, and division — are the foundation of all arithmetic.
      Each one has a different meaning and can be shown in different ways.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <subsection xml:id="num-addition">
    <title>Addition</title>
    <p>
      Addition means putting groups together or accumulating.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Example:</strong> If you have 3 apples and someone gives you 2 more, you now have <m>3 + 2 = 5</m> apples.
    </p>
    <p>
      We can show addition on a number line by jumping to the right:
    </p>
    <figure xml:id="fig-addition-number-line">
      <caption>Addition on a number line: 3 + 2</caption>
      <image width="70%">
        <description>A number line from 0 to 6, with arrows showing a jump from 0 to 3, then from 3 to 5</description>
        <latex-image>
          \begin{tikzpicture}
            \draw[-latex] (-0.5,0) -- (6.5,0);
            \foreach \x in {0,1,2,3,4,5,6} {
              \draw (\x,-0.1) -- (\x,0.1);
              \node[below] at (\x,-0.2) {\x};
            }
            \draw[->, thick, blue] (0,0.5) -- (3,0.5);
            \node[above, blue] at (1.5,0.5) {+3};
            \draw[->, thick, red] (3,0.5) -- (5,0.5);
            \node[above, red] at (4,0.5) {+2};
            \fill (0,0) circle (3pt);
            \fill (5,0) circle (3pt) node[above right] {5};
          \end{tikzpicture}
        </latex-image>
      </image>
    </figure>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="num-subtraction">
    <title>Subtraction</title>
    <p>
      Subtraction can mean taking away or finding the difference between two numbers.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Example:</strong> If you have 7 euros and spend 3 euros, you have <m>7 - 3 = 4</m> euros left.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="num-multiplication">
    <title>Multiplication</title>
    <p>
      Multiplication means repeated addition or making equal groups.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Example:</strong> If you have 4 bags with 3 apples in each bag, you have <m>4 \times 3 = 12</m> apples total.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="num-division">
    <title>Division</title>
    <p>
      Division can mean sharing equally or grouping.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Example:</strong> If you have 12 apples and want to share them equally among 4 people, each person gets <m>12 \div 4 = 3</m> apples.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <exercises xml:id="exs-operations">
    <title>Exercises</title>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-add-basic">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Calculate: <m>5 + 7</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <hint>
        <p>
          Start at 5 on a number line. Jump 7 spaces to the right.
        </p>
      </hint>
      <answer>
        <p>12</p>
      </answer>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <m>5 + 7 = 12</m>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-multiply-basic">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Calculate: <m>6 \times 4</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <hint>
        <p>
          This means 6 groups of 4. Add them up: <m>4 + 4 + 4 + 4 + 4 + 4</m>.
        </p>
      </hint>
      <answer>
        <p>24</p>
      </answer>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <m>6 \times 4 = 4 + 4 + 4 + 4 + 4 + 4 = 24</m>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

  </exercises>

</section>
